 "intro": "Clastidium, south of the Po in northern Italy, 222 BC. The Insubres and their Gaesatae allies are besieging the town. You are the consul Marcus Claudius Marcellus, riding ahead with cavalry and light infantry. The sources are brief.",
 "debrief": "In Plutarch’s account, Marcellus rode ahead with his cavalry and light troops, spread his line so as not to be surrounded, and killed King Viridomarus in single combat in front of both armies, winning the spolia opima, the rare honour of a Roman general who killed an enemy king with his own hand. The Gauls were routed, and Mediolanum was soon taken. Only three Romans in history were said to have won the spolia opima. The other choices are what-ifs.",
